> Or does NH4 aim for gameplay changes in the sake of balance, Gehennom
> lameness or whatever, and I'm not aware of that?
I don't aim for 100% gameplay compatibility; so far, though the changes
have all been minor. Changes I've made generally fall into the following
categories:

* Side effects of other changes that are too minor to worry about. For
  instance, one change I'm considering would allow the "wounded legs"
  status to time out separately for the left leg and right leg, because
  this makes the code a lot less hacky. I doubt most people would notice
  or care about the change. Likewise, in the development version (these
  changes aren't on the server yet), the "T" command is now
  interruptible by monsters appearing.

  daniel_t, who implemented the change to monster difficulty you're
  complaining about, seems to have considered it to fall into this
  category. Most of the other developers aren't so sure, and if it's
  causing problems, it's something we should seek to fix.

* Fixes to vanilla bugs. For instance, Astral Call no longer works.

* Changes to simplify the interface. For instance, at least in the
  version currently under development (this might not be on the server
  yet), the 'x' command no longer takes time; this means that players
  who want to set up two-weapon fighting no longer need to do a complex
  dance messing with the 'pushweapon' option in order to get the fastest

* Confirmations on dangerous actions. For instance, characters are now
  warned if they attempt to eat a cockatrice corpse. I don't really
  consider these to be gameplay changes (because a sufficiently good
  wrapper for 3.4.3 could implement them), but some people do.

* Compensation for interface improvements. For instance, NetHack 4 now
  remembers the previous descriptions of items and level layouts when
  you forget them to amnesia (something which anyone could do in 3.4.3
  using pencil and paper, but few people went through the trouble). In
  order to compensate for this, amnesia now also drains skill points,
  something that it doesn't do in 3.4.3. Likewise, Stormbringer now has
  the "aggravate monster" extrinsic, to compensate for it not
  automatically confirming attacks on allies.

* Reduction of situations that force the player to adopt degenerate
  behaviour even if they don't want to. The classic example of this is
  fewer than seven candles generating naturally; it's notable that this
  situation cannot be resolved without breaking at least one conduct
  (pacifist if you farm deathdrops, wishless if you wish for them,
  polypileless if you polypile for them). In NetHack 4, candles generate
  wherever there are gnomes (mostly in their inventories), so you can
  just stand around in the Mines and eventually you'll have enough
  candles even if something went badly wrong in Izchak's.

Two categories of change that we're planning to make in future
versions (most likely 4.4 onwards; 4.2.1 is the current version), but
that have not been made yet:

* Reduction of near-unavoidable deaths that aren't the player's fault,
  such as making wands used by monsters less deadly in the early game,
  and removing the risk of "deadly poison" from early-game poison
  sources so that the player has a chance to obtain poison resistance
  before they start happening.

* Reduction of late-game tedium. Pretty much everyone agrees that
  something needs to be done about the game post-Castle, but a huge
  amount of consideration and testing needs to be done in order to
  determine exactly what it is. Fixing Gehennom (and to a lesser
  extent, the Planes of Water and Fire) is not an easy task.

I'm going to avoid these categories of change for 4.3, which focuses on
getting the internals of the game sorted out. Hopefully, it should be
the close-to-vanilla-with-better-interface that many people seem to
want, and any major gameplay improvements can come after that.

